# Terminal initialization and event loop

Set up ratatui terminal with crossterm backend and basic event loop.

## Context

Before we can render PTY output, we need a working terminal UI foundation. This ticket establishes the ratatui + crossterm setup with proper cleanup on exit.

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/main.rs` — Add terminal setup and event loop

### Pattern: Terminal Setup

```rust
use std::io;
use crossterm::{
    event::{self, Event, KeyCode},
    execute,
    terminal::{disable_raw_mode, enable_raw_mode, EnterAlternateScreen, LeaveAlternateScreen},
};
use ratatui::{backend::CrosstermBackend, Terminal};
use color_eyre::eyre::Result;

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    color_eyre::install()?;

    // Setup terminal
    enable_raw_mode()?;
    let mut stdout = io::stdout();
    execute\!(stdout, EnterAlternateScreen)?;
    let backend = CrosstermBackend::new(stdout);
    let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend)?;

    // Run app
    let result = run(&mut terminal);

    // Restore terminal (always, even on error)
    disable_raw_mode()?;
    execute\!(terminal.backend_mut(), LeaveAlternateScreen)?;

    result
}

fn run(terminal: &mut Terminal<CrosstermBackend<io::Stdout>>) -> Result<()> {
    loop {
        terminal.draw(|frame| {
            // Placeholder: render a block
            let block = ratatui::widgets::Block::default()
                .title("bz")
                .borders(ratatui::widgets::Borders::ALL);
            frame.render_widget(block, frame.area());
        })?;

        // Handle input
        if event::poll(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100))? {
            if let Event::Key(key) = event::read()? {
                if key.code == KeyCode::Char('q') {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

### Pattern: Panic Hook (optional but recommended)

```rust
// In main(), before enable_raw_mode:
let original_hook = std::panic::take_hook();
std::panic::set_hook(Box::new(move |panic| {
    disable_raw_mode().unwrap();
    execute\!(io::stdout(), LeaveAlternateScreen).unwrap();
    original_hook(panic);
}));
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] `cargo run` shows a bordered box titled "bz"
- [ ] Pressing 'q' cleanly exits to normal terminal
- [ ] Ctrl+C or panic restores terminal properly (no garbled output)

## Gotchas

- Must restore terminal on ALL exit paths (success, error, panic)
- Use `execute\!` macro, not `queue\!` (immediate flush needed)

# Spawn PTY and render output

Spawn a shell in a PTY and render its output using tui-term.

## Context

This is the core proof-of-concept: can we spawn a real shell and render it in our TUI? We use portable-pty for PTY creation and tui-term/vt100 for rendering.

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/main.rs` — Add PTY spawning and rendering

### Pattern: PTY Setup with portable-pty

```rust
use portable_pty::{native_pty_system, CommandBuilder, PtySize};
use vt100::Parser;

fn spawn_pty() -> Result<(Box<dyn portable_pty::MasterPty + Send>, Box<dyn std::io::Read + Send>)> {
    let pty_system = native_pty_system();

    let pair = pty_system.openpty(PtySize {
        rows: 24,
        cols: 80,
        pixel_width: 0,
        pixel_height: 0,
    })?;

    let cmd = CommandBuilder::new("bash");
    // Or: CommandBuilder::new(std::env::var("SHELL").unwrap_or("bash".into()));

    let _child = pair.slave.spawn_command(cmd)?;

    // Get reader for PTY output
    let reader = pair.master.try_clone_reader()?;

    Ok((pair.master, reader))
}
```

### Pattern: vt100 Parser + tui-term Rendering

```rust
use tui_term::widget::PseudoTerminal;

// Create parser with terminal dimensions
let mut parser = vt100::Parser::new(24, 80, 0); // rows, cols, scrollback

// In read loop, feed bytes to parser:
let mut buf = [0u8; 4096];
let n = reader.read(&mut buf)?;
parser.process(&buf[..n]);

// In draw callback:
terminal.draw(|frame| {
    let pseudo_term = PseudoTerminal::new(parser.screen());
    frame.render_widget(pseudo_term, frame.area());
})?;
```

### Approach: Blocking Read (simplest for now)

For this ticket, use a simple blocking approach with short timeouts. We will switch to async in Phase 2.

```rust
use std::io::Read;

// Set non-blocking or use timeout
// Read in poll loop alongside event handling
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Running `cargo run` shows a bash prompt
- [ ] Shell output (e.g., typing `ls`) renders correctly
- [ ] Colors and basic formatting work (try `ls --color`)

## Gotchas

- PTY size must match parser size, or output will wrap incorrectly
- The `slave` must be kept alive or the child process dies
- `try_clone_reader()` gives us a reader we can use in a separate thread/task

# Forward keyboard input to PTY

Route keyboard events from crossterm to the PTY stdin.

## Context

The shell is running but we cannot type into it yet. We need to forward keyboard input from crossterm events to the PTY master's writer.

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/main.rs` — Add input forwarding in event loop

### Pattern: Get PTY Writer

```rust
// From the master PTY:
let mut writer = pair.master.take_writer()?;

// Writer implements std::io::Write
```

### Pattern: Convert crossterm Events to Bytes

```rust
use crossterm::event::{Event, KeyCode, KeyModifiers, KeyEvent};

fn key_to_bytes(key: KeyEvent) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
    let bytes = match key.code {
        KeyCode::Char(c) => {
            if key.modifiers.contains(KeyModifiers::CONTROL) {
                // Ctrl+letter = ASCII 1-26
                let ctrl = (c.to_ascii_lowercase() as u8) - b'a' + 1;
                vec![ctrl]
            } else {
                let mut buf = [0u8; 4];
                let s = c.encode_utf8(&mut buf);
                s.as_bytes().to_vec()
            }
        }
        KeyCode::Enter => vec![b'\r'],
        KeyCode::Backspace => vec![127], // or vec![8] depending on terminal
        KeyCode::Tab => vec![b'\t'],
        KeyCode::Esc => vec![27],
        KeyCode::Up => vec![27, b'[', b'A'],
        KeyCode::Down => vec![27, b'[', b'B'],
        KeyCode::Right => vec![27, b'[', b'C'],
        KeyCode::Left => vec![27, b'[', b'D'],
        KeyCode::Home => vec![27, b'[', b'H'],
        KeyCode::End => vec![27, b'[', b'F'],
        KeyCode::Delete => vec![27, b'[', b'3', b'~'],
        _ => return None,
    };
    Some(bytes)
}
```

### Pattern: Write to PTY

```rust
// In event loop:
if let Event::Key(key) = event::read()? {
    // Check for our quit key first
    if key.code == KeyCode::Char('q') && key.modifiers.contains(KeyModifiers::CONTROL) {
        break; // Ctrl+Q to quit (temporary, will change to leader key later)
    }

    if let Some(bytes) = key_to_bytes(key) {
        writer.write_all(&bytes)?;
    }
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Can type commands in the shell (ls, cd, etc.)
- [ ] Enter key submits commands
- [ ] Backspace works
- [ ] Arrow keys work for readline navigation
- [ ] Ctrl+C sends interrupt to shell (not to bz)

## Gotchas

- Ctrl+Q (or whatever quit key) must be handled BEFORE forwarding
- Some terminals expect different escape sequences for arrow keys
- Backspace can be 127 (DEL) or 8 (BS) depending on terminfo

# Handle terminal resize

Propagate terminal size changes to the PTY and vt100 parser.

## Context

When the user resizes their terminal window, we need to:
1. Detect the resize event (crossterm provides this)
2. Update the PTY size (so the child process knows)
3. Update the vt100 parser size (so rendering is correct)

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/main.rs` — Add resize handling in event loop

### Pattern: Detect Resize Event

```rust
use crossterm::event::{Event, KeyEvent};

// In event loop:
match event::read()? {
    Event::Key(key) => { /* handle key */ }
    Event::Resize(width, height) => {
        handle_resize(width, height, &master, &mut parser)?;
    }
    _ => {}
}
```

### Pattern: Resize PTY

```rust
use portable_pty::PtySize;

fn handle_resize(
    cols: u16,
    rows: u16,
    master: &Box<dyn portable_pty::MasterPty + Send>,
    parser: &mut vt100::Parser,
) -> Result<()> {
    // Resize PTY (this sends SIGWINCH to the child)
    master.resize(PtySize {
        rows,
        cols,
        pixel_width: 0,
        pixel_height: 0,
    })?;

    // Resize parser
    parser.set_size(rows, cols);

    Ok(())
}
```

### Initial Size

At startup, get the terminal size and use it for initial PTY/parser creation:

```rust
use crossterm::terminal::size;

let (cols, rows) = size()?;

// Use these for initial PtySize and Parser::new()
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Resize terminal window while running vim → vim redraws correctly
- [ ] Resize while running htop → htop adapts to new size
- [ ] No visual glitches or garbled output after resize

## Gotchas

- Must resize BOTH the PTY (for SIGWINCH) AND the parser (for rendering)
- Some programs need a moment to respond to SIGWINCH
- Resize events can come rapidly; debouncing might help but usually not needed

# Add TUI integration tests with ratatui-testlib

Enable automated TUI testing by integrating ratatui-testlib.

## Context

Without real PTY-based testing, we cannot automatically verify TUI behavior. ratatui-testlib spawns the app in a real PTY, sends keystrokes, and captures rendered output for assertions. This enables CI and removes manual verification friction.

## Implementation

### Dependencies to Add

```toml
[dev-dependencies]
ratatui-testlib = "0.1"
```

### Pattern: Basic Test Harness

```rust
// tests/integration.rs
use ratatui_testlib::{TuiTestHarness, Result};
use portable_pty::CommandBuilder;

#[test]
fn test_app_starts_and_quits() -> Result<()> {
    let mut harness = TuiTestHarness::new(80, 24)?;

    let mut cmd = CommandBuilder::new("./target/debug/bz");
    harness.spawn(cmd)?;

    // Give it a moment to render
    std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100));

    // Verify initial render contains expected text
    let screen = harness.screen();
    assert!(screen.contains("bz"));

    // Send quit key
    harness.send_key('q')?;

    // Verify clean exit
    let status = harness.wait()?;
    assert!(status.success());

    Ok(())
}
```

### Pattern: Test Activity Detection (Phase 3+)

```rust
#[test]
fn test_activity_detection() -> Result<()> {
    // Spawn bz with multiple channels
    // Send bell to unfocused channel
    // Verify activity indicator appears
    // Switch to that channel
    // Verify indicator clears
}
```

### CI Integration

Add to CI workflow:
```yaml
- name: Run TUI tests
  run: cargo test --test integration
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] `cargo test` runs TUI integration tests
- [ ] Tests can spawn bz, send keys, read screen
- [ ] At least one test verifies startup and quit
- [ ] Tests pass in CI (headless)

## Gotchas

- Tests need the binary built first (`cargo build` before `cargo test`)
- PTY tests may behave differently in CI vs local
- Screen assertions should be fuzzy (dont rely on exact positioning)

# Convert to async with tokio

Migrate from blocking event loop to tokio async runtime.

## Context

To read from multiple PTYs concurrently, we need async. This ticket converts the event loop to use tokio, preparing for multi-PTY support.

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/main.rs` — Convert to async runtime

### Pattern: tokio Main

```rust
use tokio;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    color_eyre::install()?;

    // Terminal setup (sync, before async)
    enable_raw_mode()?;
    // ...

    let result = run(&mut terminal).await;

    // Cleanup (sync, after async)
    disable_raw_mode()?;
    // ...

    result
}

async fn run(terminal: &mut Terminal<CrosstermBackend<io::Stdout>>) -> Result<()> {
    // ...
}
```

### Pattern: Async Event Stream

```rust
use crossterm::event::EventStream;
use futures::StreamExt;

let mut event_stream = EventStream::new();

loop {
    tokio::select\! {
        Some(Ok(event)) = event_stream.next() => {
            match event {
                Event::Key(key) => { /* handle */ }
                Event::Resize(w, h) => { /* handle */ }
                _ => {}
            }
        }
    }

    // Render
    terminal.draw(|frame| { /* ... */ })?;
}
```

### Pattern: Async PTY Reading

For now, keep PTY reading sync but wrap in spawn_blocking or use a channel:

```rust
use tokio::sync::mpsc;

let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel::<Vec<u8>>(100);

// Spawn blocking reader in background
let reader_handle = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {
    let mut buf = [0u8; 4096];
    loop {
        match reader.read(&mut buf) {
            Ok(0) => break,
            Ok(n) => {
                let _ = tx.blocking_send(buf[..n].to_vec());
            }
            Err(_) => break,
        }
    }
});

// In select\! loop:
tokio::select\! {
    Some(Ok(event)) = event_stream.next() => { /* ... */ }
    Some(data) = rx.recv() => {
        parser.process(&data);
    }
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] App runs on tokio runtime
- [ ] Input handling works as before
- [ ] PTY output still renders correctly
- [ ] No noticeable latency increase

## Gotchas

- EventStream requires `crossterm` feature `event-stream`
- Update Cargo.toml: `crossterm = { version = "0.28", features = ["event-stream"] }`
- Terminal rendering must happen on main thread (ratatui is not Send)

# Multi-PTY data structure and concurrent reads

Support multiple PTY instances with concurrent output reading.

## Context

We need to run multiple shells (one per channel eventually). This ticket adds the data structure and concurrent reading infrastructure.

## Implementation

### Files to Create/Modify

- `src/pty.rs` — New file for PTY abstraction
- `src/main.rs` — Use Pty struct, manage multiple

### Pattern: Pty Struct

```rust
// src/pty.rs
use portable_pty::{MasterPty, PtySize};
use vt100::Parser;
use tokio::sync::mpsc;

pub struct Pty {
    pub id: usize,
    pub parser: Parser,
    master: Box<dyn MasterPty + Send>,
    writer: Box<dyn std::io::Write + Send>,
    output_rx: mpsc::Receiver<Vec<u8>>,
    // Handle to the reader task (for cleanup)
    _reader_handle: tokio::task::JoinHandle<()>,
}

impl Pty {
    pub fn spawn(id: usize, rows: u16, cols: u16) -> Result<Self> {
        let pty_system = portable_pty::native_pty_system();
        let pair = pty_system.openpty(PtySize {
            rows, cols, pixel_width: 0, pixel_height: 0,
        })?;

        let cmd = portable_pty::CommandBuilder::new("bash");
        let _child = pair.slave.spawn_command(cmd)?;

        let mut reader = pair.master.try_clone_reader()?;
        let writer = pair.master.take_writer()?;
        let parser = Parser::new(rows, cols, 0);

        // Spawn reader task
        let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel(100);
        let reader_handle = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {
            let mut buf = [0u8; 4096];
            loop {
                match reader.read(&mut buf) {
                    Ok(0) => break,
                    Ok(n) => {
                        if tx.blocking_send(buf[..n].to_vec()).is_err() {
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    Err(_) => break,
                }
            }
        });

        Ok(Self {
            id,
            parser,
            master: pair.master,
            writer,
            output_rx: rx,
            _reader_handle: reader_handle,
        })
    }

    pub fn resize(&mut self, rows: u16, cols: u16) -> Result<()> {
        self.master.resize(PtySize {
            rows, cols, pixel_width: 0, pixel_height: 0,
        })?;
        self.parser.set_size(rows, cols);
        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn write(&mut self, data: &[u8]) -> Result<()> {
        self.writer.write_all(data)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn try_read(&mut self) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
        self.output_rx.try_recv().ok()
    }
}
```

### Pattern: App State with Multiple PTYs

```rust
// src/main.rs
struct App {
    ptys: Vec<Pty>,
    focused: usize,
}

impl App {
    fn focused_pty(&mut self) -> &mut Pty {
        &mut self.ptys[self.focused]
    }
}
```

### Pattern: Read from All PTYs

```rust
// In main loop, after handling events:
for pty in &mut app.ptys {
    while let Some(data) = pty.try_read() {
        pty.parser.process(&data);
    }
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Can spawn 3 PTYs at startup
- [ ] All 3 PTYs run their shells independently
- [ ] Only focused PTY is rendered (for now)
- [ ] Switching focus (next ticket) shows different shell

## Gotchas

- Each PTY needs its own reader task (they block independently)
- `try_recv()` is non-blocking; use in render loop
- Dropping Pty should clean up child process (portable-pty handles this)

# Tab switching keybinds

Add keybinds to switch between PTYs (proto-channels).

## Context

With multiple PTYs running, we need a way to switch between them. This ticket adds temporary keybinds for cycling. Later, the sidebar will be the primary navigation.

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/main.rs` — Add switching logic in event handler

### Keybinds (temporary, will be replaced by sidebar)

- `Ctrl+N` — Next PTY
- `Ctrl+P` — Previous PTY  
- `Ctrl+Q` — Quit

These are temporary. Phase 4 will introduce `Ctrl+B` leader key.

### Pattern: Cycle Focus

```rust
fn next_pty(app: &mut App) {
    app.focused = (app.focused + 1) % app.ptys.len();
}

fn prev_pty(app: &mut App) {
    if app.focused == 0 {
        app.focused = app.ptys.len() - 1;
    } else {
        app.focused -= 1;
    }
}
```

### Pattern: Handle in Event Loop

```rust
Event::Key(key) => {
    if key.modifiers.contains(KeyModifiers::CONTROL) {
        match key.code {
            KeyCode::Char('n') => next_pty(&mut app),
            KeyCode::Char('p') => prev_pty(&mut app),
            KeyCode::Char('q') => break,
            _ => forward_to_pty(&mut app, key)?,
        }
    } else {
        forward_to_pty(&mut app, key)?;
    }
}
```

### Pattern: Visual Indicator (temporary)

Show which PTY is focused in a simple status line:

```rust
terminal.draw(|frame| {
    let chunks = Layout::default()
        .direction(Direction::Vertical)
        .constraints([
            Constraint::Min(0),
            Constraint::Length(1),
        ])
        .split(frame.area());

    // PTY in main area
    let pseudo_term = PseudoTerminal::new(app.focused_pty().parser.screen());
    frame.render_widget(pseudo_term, chunks[0]);

    // Status line
    let status = format\!("PTY {}/{} | Ctrl+N/P to switch | Ctrl+Q to quit", 
        app.focused + 1, app.ptys.len());
    let status_widget = Paragraph::new(status)
        .style(Style::default().bg(Color::DarkGray));
    frame.render_widget(status_widget, chunks[1]);
})?;
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Ctrl+N cycles to next PTY
- [ ] Ctrl+P cycles to previous PTY
- [ ] Status line shows current PTY index
- [ ] Each PTY maintains its own shell state when switching away and back

## Gotchas

- Must NOT forward Ctrl+N/P to the PTY (consume them)
- Shell state persists because PTYs keep running in background

# Implement ActivityState and detection

Add activity tracking to PTYs with bell detection.

## Context

This is the proof point for the entire rewrite. We need to detect when unfocused PTYs have output and when they emit bells. This validates that owning the PTY read loop solves the activity detection problem.

## Implementation

### Files to Modify

- `src/pty.rs` — Add ActivityState
- `src/main.rs` — Update status line to show activity

### Pattern: ActivityState Enum

```rust
// src/pty.rs

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum ActivityState {
    Idle,
    Active(u32),      // u32 = bell count
    // AwaitingInput,  // Future: for Claude Code detection
}

impl Default for ActivityState {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::Idle
    }
}
```

### Pattern: Add to Pty Struct

```rust
pub struct Pty {
    pub id: usize,
    pub parser: Parser,
    pub activity: ActivityState,
    // ... rest unchanged
}
```

### Pattern: Detect Activity on Read

```rust
impl Pty {
    /// Process incoming data. Returns true if data was processed.
    pub fn process_output(&mut self, is_focused: bool) -> bool {
        let mut had_output = false;

        while let Some(data) = self.try_read() {
            had_output = true;

            // Count bells in this chunk
            let bell_count = data.iter().filter(|&&b| b == 0x07).count() as u32;

            // Update activity if not focused
            if \!is_focused {
                match &mut self.activity {
                    ActivityState::Idle => {
                        self.activity = ActivityState::Active(bell_count);
                    }
                    ActivityState::Active(count) => {
                        *count += bell_count;
                    }
                }
            }

            // Always process through parser
            self.parser.process(&data);
        }

        had_output
    }

    /// Clear activity (call when PTY becomes focused)
    pub fn clear_activity(&mut self) {
        self.activity = ActivityState::Idle;
    }
}
```

### Pattern: Clear on Focus Change

```rust
// In main, when switching PTYs:
fn switch_to_pty(app: &mut App, index: usize) {
    app.focused = index;
    app.ptys[index].clear_activity();
}
```

### Pattern: Display Activity in Status Line

```rust
// Build status showing activity per PTY
let status_parts: Vec<String> = app.ptys.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, pty)| {
    let marker = match &pty.activity {
        ActivityState::Idle => "",
        ActivityState::Active(0) => "*",
        ActivityState::Active(n) => &format\!("({})", n),
    };
    if i == app.focused {
        format\!("[PTY{}{}]", i + 1, marker)
    } else {
        format\!(" PTY{}{} ", i + 1, marker)
    }
}).collect();

let status = status_parts.join(" ");
```

### Testing Bell Detection

Run this in an unfocused PTY to test bell:
```bash
echo -e "\\a"  # or: printf "\\007"
```

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Status line shows `*` for unfocused PTYs with output
- [ ] Status line shows `(N)` for unfocused PTYs with N bells
- [ ] Switching to a PTY clears its activity indicator
- [ ] Focused PTY never shows activity (even with bells)

## Gotchas

- Bell is `0x07` (ASCII BEL), check raw bytes before vt100 parsing
- Activity must be tracked per-PTY, not globally
- Clear activity AFTER switching focus, not before

# Channel data model and TOML config

Replace raw PTY list with proper channel abstraction and config file.

## Context

Currently we have a flat list of PTYs. Channels are the user-facing concept: named workspaces that contain one or more PTYs. This ticket adds the data model and TOML configuration.

## Implementation

### Files to Create/Modify

- `src/channel.rs` — New file for Channel struct
- `src/config.rs` — New file for config parsing
- `src/main.rs` — Use channels instead of raw PTYs
- `bz.toml` — Example config file
- `Cargo.toml` — Add serde, toml dependencies

### Dependencies to Add

```toml
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
toml = "0.8"
dirs = "5"  # For finding config file
```

### Pattern: Config File Structure

```toml
# bz.toml (in project root or ~/.config/bz/config.toml)

[[channel]]
name = "fort-nix"
cwd = "/home/dev/Projects/fort-nix"
command = "bash"  # or "tmux new-session -A -s fort-nix"

[[channel]]
name = "exocortex"
cwd = "/home/dev/Projects/exocortex"

[[channel]]
name = "wicket"
cwd = "/home/dev/Projects/wicket"
```

### Pattern: Config Structs

```rust
// src/config.rs
use serde::Deserialize;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
pub struct Config {
    #[serde(default)]
    pub channel: Vec<ChannelConfig>,
}

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
pub struct ChannelConfig {
    pub name: String,
    pub cwd: Option<String>,
    #[serde(default = "default_command")]
    pub command: String,
}

fn default_command() -> String {
    std::env::var("SHELL").unwrap_or_else(|_| "bash".into())
}

impl Config {
    pub fn load() -> Result<Self> {
        // Try ./bz.toml first, then ~/.config/bz/config.toml
        let paths = [
            std::path::PathBuf::from("bz.toml"),
            dirs::config_dir()
                .map(|p| p.join("bz/config.toml"))
                .unwrap_or_default(),
        ];

        for path in paths {
            if path.exists() {
                let content = std::fs::read_to_string(&path)?;
                return Ok(toml::from_str(&content)?);
            }
        }

